Run threads on parked workers

Each spawn starts a Web Worker, which loads the module's shim and
instantiates the module before the thread runs, and a spawn from a worker
waits on the main thread to start it, behind whatever that thread is busy
with. Here a worker whose thread has finished parks, and the next spawn
with the same worker script and stack size, from any thread, hands its
thread to the worker parked last; a worker left idle long enough ends. A
worker parks before its thread's joiner is woken, so a spawn right after a
join finds it. A scoped thread is counted before it is handed over, since
a parked worker can finish it before the spawn returns. A reused worker
keeps the name and thread-locals of the thread it first ran.

A thread must leave nothing queued on its worker's event loop, such as a
promise, a timer or a message handler: a parked worker does not return to
the loop, so that work would wait until the worker ends. A parked worker
the browser has ended, for example one that ran out of memory, stays
listed, and a thread handed to it never runs.

